[{:seon.task/name "a-run-past-its-cap-ends"
  :seon.task/plan "Seen in the related A/B (worktree a-text-written-on-a-branch-is-embedded-there, round on-2, 2026-10-25, deepseek, :seon.config.agent/max-turns 30): the run of the task an-unrun-proof-is-unknown-in-the-context recorded 29 screen evals and 36 model evals, no :seon.end fact, status :running and its agent :busy for over an hour after its last turn, with an empty stderr (runs/store-agents/main/a1.err, 0 bytes); its root answered every other request. The round's stats are docs/vector.md; the store was erased with the round (an experiment store), so what the agent was evaluating is not kept. Not known: whether the last eval hung (a form with no bound; every form has :seon.config.agent/form-ticks now), the model call never answered (seon.model's edge, no timeout by design: a never-answered call is a recorded stall: docs/errors.md#nothing-crashes-and-nothing-hangs), or the loop missed its cap. The first step reproduces it with the scripted provider at the cap (a reply whose eval never returns; a call never answered) and reads the agent's state over the server while it is busy (seon_agents, the agent's :fx-pending, its last eval record), so the stall is a fact on the run (blocked, stalled) and the run ends rather than holding its agent: never a clock deciding; the fact names what it waits on."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.agent-test/a-run-waiting-past-its-cap-says-on-what}}
 {:seon.task/name "an-agent-that-breaks-its-own-render-is-told-and-goes-on"
  :seon.task/plan "Seen 2026-11-02 by the context lab's curation pilot (a scripted replay of task 618a903ed3 on a reverse-patched tree; repro in /private/tmp/seon-l2r/618a903ed3/repro-no-end/README.md): turn 3 made three calls; edit! of seon.ctx/members-map was refused, the next call's edit! of seon.ctx/join applied anyway (join now calls (members-map pairs flag)), and the agent's next context render threw; its following turn threw the same way and the run ended :no-end, \"two turns in a row threw\" (seon.turn/work!). The first cause found then, that a call answering an error value did not stop the reply's later calls, is superseded: `seon.repl/run-calls` now runs every call of a reply whatever came before it, so one failure does not cost the reply's other evals, and each call's result reads in order after the one before. What stands: an agent that breaks code its own turn loop runs (seon.ctx, the render: Seon improves itself) can no longer be sent anything, since the request is drawn by that code. A turn that threw is told, as far as it can be (`seon.turn/turn-error!` writes the error as a message from seon on its branch, which the next turn's request reads), but when the request itself cannot be drawn there is no model turn to read the message, and work! ends the run :error on the second throw in a row. Correct: a turn whose request cannot be drawn sends the previous request as it was sent (the prefix the cache already holds, seon.ctx.wire/request) with the error as one message printed by the one printer, so the agent reads why and fixes its branch; the run ends only by the existing caps (max-turns), never because the agent's own code broke its page. Owner's ruling 2026-10-28 (never crash, never hang: errors go to whoever can fix them). Design to be shown to the owner before it is built."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.turn-test/a-turn-whose-page-cannot-be-drawn-sends-its-error}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-branch-asks-whether-a-test-is-a-process-test"
  :seon.task/plan "Dogfood 0.7, form: (seon.agent/branch-graph) at the REPL is an arity error (it takes db, base db and inputs), and no public my.agent function answers \"is this test a process test on my branch\", which decides whether (my.agent/prove) runs it or defers it. Add that answer to my.agent beside tests-reaching and deferred (status of a test: :process, :plain), reading the reach graph the test plan reads. Proof: (my.agent/tests-reaching ..) results carry :process? for a test reaching a process var. An attempt (a Seon agent's merge on a curated root, 2026-11-10, 67893381bb) made my.agent/tests-reaching answer {:test :process?} from (:process (agent/test-plan ...)); Tests' review: NOT done. Its proof seon.agent-test/tests-reaching-says-which-tests-are-process-tests is red ({false 2}): the scratch test calling seon.test-store/binary-sh is not flagged :process?, so the gap is upstream, why a scratch namespace's form using a process var gets no :process? in the branch graph (the form's process-var use not analyzed, or judges?/graph-index treating scratch namespaces differently). And test-plan digests every test (~165M ticks) to read a few: the cheap form indexes only the tests found, (program/graph-index g in (zipmap (map str ts) (map vector ts))). And the proof was green in its shard of two proves and red by name: it depends on state it does not make (the process store's), which the next attempt's proof must make itself."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.agent-test/tests-reaching-says-which-tests-are-process-tests}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-merge-turn-is-a-turn-with-only-the-merge-left"
  :seon.task/plan "Reproduction (2026-11-10, Context, a test root from jolt/request-launch, store .worktrees/request-launch/runs/store): request/add-a-function-demo-536871029's agent never named a proof; two siblings merged demo.math/triple first, so its status held an unmerged entry; after merging main, root closed it held 'ended merge-failed (3 turns without green > :seon.config.merge/max-merge-turns 2): no-proofs'. Cause: seon.turn counts a merge turn on every turn of a merging agent while any unmerged entry is open, whatever else the done rule says, so the task's own work counted as merging. Plan: a turn counts as a merge turn only when the done rule's reasons, minus :unmerged, are empty (the unmerged entries are all that stands between the task and green); any other open reason is the task's own work, counted against :seon.config.agent/max-turns only, and the agent is told its first reason. It deletes the whole?/merge-step? case in the count: a merge task's only reason is its unmerged entries. Proof, red first: a task with :no-proofs and an unmerged entry runs past max-merge-turns turns and is not closed merge-failed; once its proof is named and green, its next turns with only :unmerged left count, and past the limit it is closed."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{}}
 {:seon.task/name "the-calls-seon-makes-for-an-agent-are-stored-evals"
  :seon.task/plan "The owner, 2026-11-11: 'I just want it to be deterministic and to work'. What exists: at each request the render makes again the calls Seon makes for the agent (its ctx at the first point, the page, the diff between two points), so a change to a render's code moves the bytes of old turns in the request, and a finished agent whose own render drew part of its page cannot be shown once its process is gone. Plan: seon.turn writes each such call at the turn's open as an eval with the attributes an agent's own eval has (its form, its answer, its basis), `:seon.eval/by` the system's entity, its intent and message read from the metadata of the function called; the request and the page read the stored answer (docs/ctx.md#what-the-model-reads-and-what-the-page-renders). No new attribute. An example session then keeps them too: they are what the model read at that turn, where today an example keeps the model's evals and drops the harness's (seon.ctx.session/session-tx). Deletes: the render-time calls in seon.ctx.wire and their second producer on the page. Cost: one write a turn, of the diff's size. Proof, red first: the same store gives the same request bytes before and after a render's code changes; a compaction and a wiped process included; a recorded request is equal byte for byte. Measured 2026-11-11 (Optimization, the stored profile of seon.render-cost-test/a-cold-request-draws-what-the-model-reads, 74k calls): seon.print.select is 36% of a cold request's calls (each record's eval value printed again at every request) and seon.ratom 33% (a view and a watcher per deref in a request nothing watches after). So the stored eval keeps its printed text, made once at its turn, and a request reads it; and a cold request reads untracked."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.turn-test/a-turns-own-calls-are-stored-and-read-back}}
 {:seon.task/name "the-turn-loops-own-eval-is-not-sent-to-the-model"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner: Context. Found 2026-11-12 by Web, in one real agent turn on jolt/debuglabel's own system (root's session, deepseek). The turn loop's own eval, (seon.turn/work! {:task \"request/root\" :max-turns 40}), was sent to the model as a user message {:by \"root\" :code ...}: root is both the agent and the author of that eval, so the rule that sends an eval written by the agent itself sends it. The model then spent a whole call replying that its turn was done, and work! ran a second time (:max-turns 50) with that reply. The ordinary way: an eval's origin, recorded when it is made, says whether the model wrote it; the request reads that, not the author. Plan: the request leaves out an eval whose origin is the turn loop's own, whoever wrote it; the page folds it with the system's other evals. Proof to write, beside seon.ctx.wire-test's eval-placement tests: root's request after one turn holds no work! eval, and one request makes one call for a one-eval answer."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{}}]
